  "structure": {
    "block_count": 88,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "What Types of Fish Are Safe to Eat During Pregnancy?",
        "text_sha256": "3c61fac0283e8e1af4bf953c5690ab8acd6f7d3a94a2feb7cbb3ec6542133594",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Light copy updates, updates to sources, and fact-checked for accuracy.",
        "text_sha256": "03ab6ec71ffcc7c2416587f4ad58dc4805f0a11bd13223d387ea9b28fc87f850",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "fish pregnancy",
        "text_sha256": "6ce3b26433c0155e4209057042aca192357e70b9bdd7c213ea3576a6e2b031d9",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Fish is one of the best foods to eat when pregnant, but some types are off limits. Here's what you need to know about seafood during pregnancy, from the benefits for you and baby to what’s safe to eat and what’s not.",
        "text_sha256": "a80547f4d78b839704c67c6b711204791346b49f406e6da2f405faaab88a076f",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you’re unsure of the rules on fish and pregnancy, you’re not alone: There’s been plenty of conflicting views over the years. Fish is heart healthy! But wait, it also contains mercury. Fish is loaded with baby-friendly DHA! But not so fast — it also has PCBs.",
        "text_sha256": "5f742545fe761000670d5748095f34252bd7b78646eba46a077ddef9eafb10a6",
        "word_count": 47
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "So what’s the real dish on fish during pregnancy? Here’s the lowdown on what’s safe and what’s not when it comes to seafood.",
        "text_sha256": "fe7fbf40aa52719edf3b44fddcfa918456ba5ee5c57d7c08ecf84f5a7afa9044",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Key Takeaways",
        "text_sha256": "cc3f7f0f4513969e418b56fb93ec0876fd96dd6498395b04301f70dbddff8a45",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Fish is safe — and recommended — during pregnancy: Aim for 8 to 12 ounces (that's about two to three servings) per week of low-mercury seafood to support your baby’s brain development, growth, heart health, and more.",
        "text_sha256": "1ae652b97c112168e9ed50800d39d3386c0e005cdd1573277c9a2f5bddf973fe",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Avoid high-mercury fish and limit certain types when you're expecting. Skip shark, swordfish, king mackerel, marlin, bigeye tuna, orange roughy, and Gulf tilefish; limit certain others (like albacore tuna, halibut, snapper) to one serving per week.",
        "text_sha256": "7917f76afce2699693d143d43c2fb3cd7271000719d6a0aa840b63e11bdc90c8",
        "word_count": 36
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Always cook your seafood thoroughly. Stick to low-mercury picks like salmon (preferably wild), shrimp, cod, pollock, and canned light tuna; avoid raw or undercooked seafood, and cook it to 145°F to reduce bacteria and parasite risks.",
        "text_sha256": "54bcbcc5bd40888d6d6e3f2676a050b8f447c3f9bbe796397060a81db2d0cb4a",
        "word_count": 36
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Is fish safe during pregnancy?",
        "text_sha256": "8f1613b85155fc1de4e11628243a0dcddd3ec20ee8b77ad7d4d478531f37b5df",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Eating enough of the right types of seafood is not only healthy but recommended for both you and your baby when you're pregnant.",
        "text_sha256": "e318ed633d7e821972c29e5fc98e353d2380ada31307da37c1cfacbbff9ac08d",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Pregnant and breastfeeding women should eat 8 to 12 ounces (that's two to three servings) of low-mercury fish every week, according to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists. Trusted SourceAmerican College of Obstetricians and GynecologistsHealthy Eating During PregnancySee All Sources [1]",
        "text_sha256": "c3469135cb01321ca385fe7e6e3ebca8a67af6239968ce89ed399ab18d841f50",
        "word_count": 42
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Trusted SourceAmerican College of Obstetricians and GynecologistsHealthy Eating During PregnancySee All Sources",
        "text_sha256": "66757a57248c6d44b25625744fff222db7103bb4cd62666fd4140631df9b9b64",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "What types of fish should you avoid during pregnancy?",
        "text_sha256": "69e73062267b0e14cd0d99f03de55a90b897dd75265e594e3ba25d79e63d85f8",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Though the benefits of fish are many, you should still avoid a few types during pregnancy. Some — particularly large, ocean-faring, predator-types — contain high levels of mercury, a distinctly baby-unfriendly toxin. Others — especially those that live in polluted lakes and rivers — can harbor polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs), chemicals you definitely don't want to feed a fetus or an infant.",
        "text_sha256": "1c0c30b9861e78bc9162cd7f00ec2ed96e2dc4e86227d819e7fda161abad8fc0",
        "word_count": 61
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "To play it safe, avoid or limit the following fish while you're pregnant and nursing:",
        "text_sha256": "4f6f762d13fbae1e0f55f2500d078a594e7d48fda449c32c2fb5390f10689754",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Fish to avoid during pregnancy",
        "text_sha256": "ae2b8e32619877aef31b11580dee65a5206db57fba13f0dc4cabc2a9b1ca9e95",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It's best to avoid these seven types of fish, which are all higher in mercury:",
        "text_sha256": "5f0380e96bf42ddaf5c474bde1ddf848bd49cc8a4b6e8c0b023d004e481c64fa",
        "word_count": 15
